<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#tigrinya-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tigrinya language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tigrinya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tigrinya is a Semitic language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, spoken in the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> and in the Tigray region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. It descends from the same branch as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geez">Ge'ez</a>, the ancient language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a>, and is written in the Ge'ez script. It is one of the main working languages of Eritrea.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tigrinya-language.jpg" alt="Tigrinya language" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MarginalCost · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eritrea_Ethiopia_locator.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
